# `core::hash` -- a value's own contribution to a hash-based collection's
# bucket index (`std::hash_map::HashMap`/`std::hash_set::HashSet`).
# Deliberately its own small file, the same reason `core::default` is:
# reused wherever hashing matters, not just by the two collections that
# need it first.
#
# The only real contract: two values that compare equal (`cmp::Eq::equals`)
# must produce the same hash -- the reverse isn't required (a collision
# between unequal values is always allowed, just ideally rare). Every
# primitive implementation lives in `numerics.omg`/`strings.omg`'s own
# existing `for`-blocks (see their own doc comments for why it can't live
# here instead -- `for`-attachment is restricted to `core`, and a target
# type gets exactly one `for` block, globally).
#
# `HashMap<K: Hash, V>`/`HashSet<T: Hash>` bound only on this -- not a
# combined `Hash`-and-`Eq` spec, even though a bucket lookup needs both.
# A generic parameter only ever carries a single formal bound (see
# `GenericParam`'s own doc comment), and every realistic key type (every
# `core`-provided primitive, and any user type meant to be hashable at
# all) already provides `cmp::Eq::equals` as a matter of course, the same
# structural expectation `Ord`'s own default methods already lean on --
# so `key.equals(other)` inside `HashMap`'s own body stays duck-typed,
# unbound, exactly like any other structural call in this language (see
# `docs/08-specs.md`: "an unbound generic parameter still works purely by
# duck-typing"). A concrete `K` that doesn't actually implement `Eq`
# simply fails at that call site once substituted in, the same way it
# would for any other duck-typed method call -- no separate bound needed
# to catch it.

exposed spec Hash {
    hash(*self) => u64;
}
